Drop the GNU ", ##__VA_ARGS__" token pasting from MR_fail and MR_assert.

clang -pedantic, which CRAN's r-devel-linux-x86_64-debian-clang flavor
uses for C++, reports it as -Wgnu-zero-variadic-macro-arguments, and
R CMD check counts that as a significant warning. Every call site in the
vendored subset passes at least one argument after the format string, so
plain __VA_ARGS__ expands identically. Found in Stage 1 (pedrobtz/zufft#16,
the clang23 leg). Silencing it with a pragma would draw R's own NOTE about
diagnostic-suppressing pragmas.

--- a/infra/error_handling.h	2026-10-13 03:10:51
+++ b/infra/error_handling.h	2026-10-13 03:10:51
@@ -104,7 +104,7 @@
 /// passed arguments.
 #define MR_fail(...) \
   do { \
-    ::ducc0::detail_error_handling::fail__(DUCC0_ERROR_HANDLING_LOC_, "\n", ##__VA_ARGS__, "\n"); \
+    ::ducc0::detail_error_handling::fail__(DUCC0_ERROR_HANDLING_LOC_, "\n", __VA_ARGS__, "\n"); \
     } while(0)
 
 /// If \a cond is false, throws a std::runtime_error containing the code
@@ -112,7 +112,7 @@
 #define MR_assert(cond,...) \
   do { \
     if (cond); \
-    else { MR_fail("Assertion failure\n", ##__VA_ARGS__); } \
+    else { MR_fail("Assertion failure\n", __VA_ARGS__); } \
     } while(0)
 
 }}
